00:00:10.000 --> 00:01:08.000
What do millennials want? Independence, work-life balance, recognition. When studying the latest generation to enter adulthood, psychology professor and author, Gene Twenge, came across some surprising findings. In this episode, she talks about how and why millennials are changing the meaning of individualism in today's society. I'm Audrey Hamilton, and this is speaking of psychology. Gene Twenge is a professor of psychology at San Diego State University and the author of the best-selling books, Generation Me, and The Narcissism Epidemic. As a consultant, she advises companies and organizations on the differences between generations, the boomers, Gen Xers, and Millennials. Welcome, Dr. Twangy. Thank you.
千禧一代想要什么？独立、工作与生活的平衡、认可。在研究最新一代步入成年的人群时，心理学教授兼作家吉恩·特温格（Gene Twenge）发现了一些令人惊讶的结果。在本期节目中，她将探讨千禧一代如何以及为何在当今社会改变了个人主义的含义。我是奥黛丽·汉密尔顿（Audrey Hamilton），这是《心理学谈话》（Speaking of Psychology）。吉恩·特温格是圣地亚哥州立大学的心理学教授，也是畅销书《我的一代》（Generation Me）和《自恋流行病》（The Narcissism Epidemic）的作者。作为顾问，她为企业和组织提供有关不同代际之间差异的建议，包括婴儿潮一代、X世代和千禧一代。欢迎您，特温格博士。谢谢。

2
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Millennials, what makes them different from previous generations? What makes them similar? So let's talk about some of the differences first. So millennials tend to have very positive views. of themselves and are very optimistic about their expectations for their lives. And they're more likely to say that they're above average compared to their peers. And they tend to score higher on other measures of positive self-feas, like self-esteem and even narcissism. At the same time, they are more tolerant and less prejudicial than previous generations. So they support same-sex marriage in a much higher rate than other generations, for example.
千禧一代，是什么让他们与前几代人不同？又是什么让他们相似？让我们先谈谈一些差异。千禧一代往往对自己有非常积极的看法，对生活充满乐观的期望。他们更有可能认为自己比同龄人更优秀。在其他衡量积极自我评价的指标上，如自尊甚至自恋，他们往往得分更高。与此同时，他们比前几代人更具包容性，偏见更少。例如，他们支持同性婚姻的比率远高于其他代际。

3
00:01:51.000 --> 00:02:39.000
And some things we're doing right now, they have a much more egalitarian view of gender roles compared to what, say, boomers did when they were young back in the 1960s and 70s. And, of course, not everything is going to change over the generations. Millennials are just as likely as previous generations. For example, to want to get married and have a family. They're similar in a lot of their goals and values. that there's also some fairly distinct differences in the way they see the world. And they tend to, as a very general rule, be more focused on themselves and less focused on things outside themselves compared to the way boomers and Gen Xers were at the same age.
目前我们正在做的一些事情是，与20世纪60年代和70年代年轻时的婴儿潮一代相比，他们对性别角色的看法要平等得多。当然，并非所有事情都会随着代际更替而改变。例如，千禧一代和前几代人一样，都希望结婚并组建家庭。他们在许多目标和价值观上是相似的。但他们看待世界的方式也存在相当明显的差异。总的来说，与同龄时的婴儿潮一代和X世代相比，他们往往更关注自己，而较少关注自身以外的事物。

4
00:02:39.000 --> 00:03:19.000
And as you said, they do tend to view themselves positively. Can you explain a little bit more about this finding? Is it related to upbringing? Is it society in general? You know, where is that coming from? Yeah. So the positive self-fuse, more than likely, are rooted in the cultural movement toward more individualism. So in cross-cultural psychology, people talk about countries like the U.S. being very high in individualism, more focused on the self-and-sexual rules and less on social roles in the society, compared to a culture like Japan, which is less self-focused and has more rigid social rules and more focus on,
正如您所说，他们确实倾向于积极地看待自己。您能多解释一下这个发现吗？这与成长环境有关吗？还是与整个社会有关？您知道，这源于哪里？是的。这种积极的自我评价很可能植根于向个人主义发展的文化运动。在跨文化心理学中，人们谈论美国等国家时，认为其个人主义程度很高，更关注自我和个人规则，而较少关注社会规则，这与日本等文化形成对比，后者较少以自我为中心，拥有更僵化的社会规则，并且更多地关注……

5
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:49.000
on others, basically. So what I'm trying to look at as the big picture is how has the culture of the U.S. changed over time. And the theme that really comes up over and over is that individualism has increased. And it's not just this generation. It shows up across people of all ages. It shows up in our language use. So one of my favorite recent studies, we looked at the Google Books database over time.
基本上是关注他人。所以我试图从宏观角度观察的是美国文化如何随时间变化。真正反复出现的主题是个人主义的增强。这不仅仅是这一代人的特征，它出现在所有年龄段的人群中。它体现在我们的语言使用中。我最近最喜欢的研究之一是，我们观察了谷歌图书数据库随时间的变化。

6
00:03:49.000 --> 00:04:05.000
and found that the use of I, me, and mine increased while the use of we and us went down. But the biggest change was in you. The use of you actually quadrupled and all the second person pronoun. So you think about that in a book. If you're reading a book and it says you, it's much more individualized.
发现“我”（I）、“我的”（me, mine）的使用频率增加了，而“我们”（we, us）的使用频率下降了。但最大的变化在于“你”（you）。“你”这个第二人称代词的使用频率实际上翻了四倍。所以你在书中思考一下。如果你正在读一本书，里面出现了“你”，它会显得更加个人化。

7
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:36.000
It's the author having a conversation with the reader. And it echoes some of the self-help language of your best life. So this is a trend that really goes beyond just the millennials. They reflect this trend. so much because this is the only world that they have ever known. So that's why I call them Generation Me, because they have never known a world that put duty before self. And as you mentioned, this also plays into the idea that more recent generations are more
这是作者在与读者进行对话。它呼应了一些关于“活出最好的自己”的自助语言。所以这不仅仅是千禧一代的趋势。他们之所以如此强烈地反映了这一趋势，是因为这是他们所知的唯一世界。这就是为什么我称他们为“我的一代”，因为他们从未经历过将责任置于自我之上的世界。正如您提到的，这也与最近几代人更……

